><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250708111036+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250708111039+10'00'" name="44ad2d9e-7b3f-4450-9ad8-7f349e518146" page="7" coords="48.018800,257.075000,298.695000,257.075000,48.018800,245.656000,298.695000,245.656000,48.018800,245.057000,298.725000,245.066000,48.018800,233.638000,298.725000,233.638000,48.018800,233.104000,298.723000,233.104000,48.018800,221.675000,298.723000,221.675000,48.018800,221.076000,298.693000,221.076000,48.018800,209.657000,298.693000,209.657000,48.018800,209.056000,298.748000,209.056000,48.018800,197.637000,298.748000,197.637000,48.018800,197.095000,298.716000,197.095000,48.018800,185.676000,298.716000,185.676000,48.018800,185.075000,298.692000,185.075000,48.018800,173.656000,298.692000,173.656000,48.018800,173.057000,61.385100,173.057000,48.018800,161.638000,61.385100,161.638000,69.958100,173.057000,93.916700,173.057000,69.958100,161.638000,93.916700,161.638000,102.500000,173.057000,117.900000,173.057000,102.500000,161.638000,117.900000,161.638000,126.481000,173.057000,172.099000,173.057000,126.481000,161.638000,172.099000,161.638000,180.680000,173.057000,238.682000,173.057000,180.680000,161.638000,238.682000,161.638000,247.294000,173.057000,282.547000,173.057000,247.294000,161.638000,282.547000,161.638000,291.117000,173.057000,298.707000,173.057000,291.117000,161.638000,298.707000,161.638000,48.018800,161.095000,298.748000,161.095000,48.018800,149.676000,298.748000,149.676000,48.018800,149.076000,298.702000,149.076000,48.018800,137.657000,298.702000,137.657000,48.018800,137.057000,298.731000,137.057000,48.018800,125.638000,298.731000,125.638000,48.018800,125.094000,186.448000,125.094000,48.018800,113.675000,186.448000,113.675000" rect="47.661700,113.319000,299.105000,257.432000" subject="Comment on Text" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>The current research identified that expert coaches rely on&#xD;their </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:italic"
>experiential knowledge, temporal factors, seeing the&#xD;athlete in context</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>, and knowing </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:italic"
>what they can work with</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>,&#xD;to make “gut instinct” decisions during TID. These informational&#xD;sources can be understood through the application of&#xD;an ecological dynamics framework, allowing for idiosyncrasies&#xD;and contextual complexities for both the athlete and&#xD;the coach and accounting for changing opinions in&#xD;a dynamic environment. An understanding of expert coach&#xD;intuition is an important addition to this field of research as&#xD;coaches have an important, yet often underutilised role&#xD;within TID research and practice.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="7" rect="609.449000,165.075000,793.449000,257.075000"
/></highlight
